## Does Insurance or Medicare Cover Laser Toenail Fungus Treatment?

Your insurer will almost certainly call this cosmetic. That is the honest answer to the coverage question, and most clinic pages will not tell you that directly.

Most private insurance plans do not cover <strong>laser treatment for toenail fungus cost</strong> because they classify laser nail therapy as a cosmetic or investigational procedure. This classification is not arbitrary  it reflects the fact that the FDA has cleared laser devices for temporary improvement of nail appearance, not for fungicidal treatment, and that the clinical evidence for complete cure is genuinely mixed.

Medicare does not routinely cover laser toenail fungus treatment. The exceptions are narrow: if the infection causes documented pain, limits function, or creates secondary medical risk for a qualifying patient (particularly those with diabetes or compromised circulation), Medicare may cover debridement or other medically necessary interventions  but the laser procedure itself remains outside standard coverage in most cases. Patients with Medicare Advantage plans should contact their specific plan, as benefit structures vary.

The CPT billing code to reference when querying your insurer is 0240T, which covers low-level laser treatment for onychomycosis. A small number of plans apply benefits for this code with prior authorisation. It is worth one call to confirm before assuming the answer is no  but assume no unless your insurer confirms otherwise in writing.

One approach worth exploring: if you have a condition such as diabetes that creates medical risk from an untreated nail infection, your podiatrist may be able to document medical necessity in a way that shifts how your insurer views the claim. This is not a guarantee, and it applies to a subset of patients, but it is a legitimate conversation to have at your consultation.

