## Using HSA or FSA Funds for Laser Treatment

Here is what most people do not realise: even when insurance will not pay, your pre-tax money can.

<strong>Laser treatment for toenail fungus cost</strong> qualifies as an eligible medical expense under IRS guidelines when performed by a podiatrist for a diagnosed case of onychomycosis (ICD-10 code B35.1). That means Health Savings Account (HSA) and Flexible Spending Account (FSA) funds can be used to cover the cost. Health Reimbursement Arrangements (HRAs) are also typically eligible.

The practical implication is a real reduction in net cost. At a 22% tax bracket, a £1,200 treatment costs you approximately £936 in after-tax equivalent spending. At a 37% bracket, the same treatment costs approximately £756. That is a meaningful difference, not a marginal one.

A few practical notes: limited-purpose FSAs (designed for dental and vision only) and dependent care FSAs do not cover toenail fungus treatment. Standard HSA and FSA accounts do. Ask your plan administrator to confirm eligibility for your specific account before treatment. Most clinics accept HSA and FSA cards directly at payment; some require a receipt and reimbursement form instead.

