## Is Laser Treatment for Toenail Fungus Actually Worth the Cost?

The answer is neither a yes nor a no. It depends on which evidence you weight, and the two versions of the evidence are more different than clinic websites suggest.

The case for laser is real. A meta-analysis published in the journal Medicine, covering 35 studies and 1,723 patients, found an overall mycological cure rate of approximately 63%  meaning lab-confirmed clearance of the fungal infection, not just improved nail appearance. Mycological cure rate is the relevant metric here: it measures whether the fungus is actually gone, as opposed to whether the nail looks better. CO2 lasers showed cure rates near 74%; Nd:YAG systems showed approximately 63%. Compared to topical treatments, which often show efficacy rates below 30% for established infections, those numbers represent a genuine advantage.

The case against laser is also real. Randomised controlled trials  the more rigorous study design  show considerably more inconsistent results. A PubMed systematic review of RCTs specifically for dermatophyte toenail onychomycosis found combined clinical cure rates of 13 to 26% depending on laser type, with one study reporting a mycological cure rate of 0% for patients. Several trials found no statistically significant difference between laser treatment and comparison groups. As of 2025, the FDA classification of laser devices for onychomycosis remains "mild temporary improvement in clear nail"  not fungicidal clearance. That classification matters to every insurer that calls this cosmetic.

The honest position sits between the two: laser treatment has real evidence of benefit in some patients and some study designs, and real evidence of limited benefit in others. It is not the "game-changing" solution clinic marketing presents, and it is not the total waste of money that the most sceptical voices claim. For patients who cannot take oral antifungals safely  those with liver conditions, kidney issues, or drug interactions  laser is a legitimate clinical option. For patients who can take oral medication, the cost-effectiveness calculation is less clear.

The recurrence risk is also part of the worth-it equation. Even in cases of successful clearance, reinfection is common enough that it should be factored into the decision alongside the upfront cost.

